> | F1 | High; Task 236 | Historical records and real error outcomes can still become false `completed` receipts. |
  | F2 | High; pre-existing backend used by Task 241 | Stored credentials are attached to caller-selected URLs/protocol
s without binding to the saved provider. |
  | F3 | Medium; pre-existing backend | Upstream model IDs can reflect a secret into test responses; universal zero-lea
kage claim fails. |
  | F4 | High; Task 247 | keepID checking is optional and unwired in production; identifier presence does not conserve 
anchor content. |
  | F5 | Medium; pre-existing recovery boundary | A torn effect-journal tail aborts construction after the session leas
e is acquired, without construction rollback. |
  | F6 | Medium; pre-existing deletion boundary | Non-test production deletion outside the recycle seam remains; broad 
deletion-compliance claim is false. |
  | F7 | Medium; Task 246 validation/control gap | IME fallback only stops propagation; it does not cancel implicit for
m submission. |
  | F8 | Medium; Task 246 incomplete recovery | SSE reconnect refreshes metadata, not missed conversation/tool deltas. 
|
  
  ### 1. Thin core discipline
> **F1 — false completion remains reachable.** `apps/local-server/src/routes/activityRoutes.ts:160–164` treats any posi
tive historical `recordCount` with no outcome as completed. `server.ts:397` supplies indexed sessions; no historical la
st-run projection is added. After daemon restart, a crashed, failed or partially written nonempty session can therefore
 appear completed. Offline probe returns `completed / source=inferred` for three records with no terminal evidence.
  
> Additionally `server.ts:402–407` checks `lastTurnEnd.kind === 'failure'`; actual core/application turn failure is **`
error`**, and exhaustion is `budget` (`ExecutionLoop.ts:194–195`; server `turnStatusCode()` also recognizes `error`). T
his branch never recognizes ordinary model/tool failures. Even the last success is selected without checking for a late
r unfinished turn. The route drops `errorDetail` rather than emitting a failure summary (`activityRoutes.ts:171–184`). 
Task 236 tests inject already-correct `lastRunStatus` values and do not challenge these production mappings. Fix the sh
ared outcome projection from the latest run/turn evidence; remove positive-record-count success inference.
  
  **Journal control that does work:** the implementation is `packages/runtime/src/executor/EffectJournal.ts`, **not** t
he requested `packages/application/src/journal/` path. `:17–20` fsync intent/outcome; `:26–38` blocks unknown writes, c
hanged invocation identities and already observed calls, allowing read-only new attempts. `Executor.ts:144–147,182` cal
ls journal-before-dispatch, consumes the permit, executes, then records outcome. `compose.ts:358–363` wires the shared 
hooks. `fault-injection-gate.test.ts:55–135` has seven deterministic boundary cases, and `EffectJournal.test.ts:16–45` 
has three more. These simulate restart by constructing a fresh journal, rather than killing a real process during a par
tial write.
  
  **F5 — crash recovery is not universally clean.** `EffectJournal.ts:14` parses every nonempty JSONL line without torn
-tail handling. The offline probe writes only a partial intent to a new temporary file and observes `SyntaxError`. `com
pose.ts:280` opens Session (acquiring its lease); `:358` constructs EffectJournal, before a composed harness/close meth
od is returned. There is no enclosing rollback closing that session on this construction failure. `SessionController.cr
eate()` only catches registry registration failures after successful composition (`:130–151`). A malformed/torn journal
 can thus leave an inaccessible live-pid lease until process exit; subsequent same-process opens are rejected (`package
s/core/src/session/Session.ts:90–115`). This is fail-closed against replay, but fails clean recovery/no-dangling-lock a
cceptance. Preserve/quarantine the tail and add composition rollback outside core; test a torn tail after an fsynced va
lid intent and same-process reopen.
  
  The ordinary abort race and persisted terminal handling support cooperative cancellation (`packages/core/src/agent-lo
op/InterruptController.ts:22–84` and AgentLoop's interrupt race); they do not prove every asynchronous journal, observe
r or close operation is bounded. `compose.ts:724–731` awaits reflection/storage before Session.close without a finalize
r around the whole teardown. No blanket no-hang/no-lock guarantee is awarded.
  
  ### 3. Physical deletion and recycle receipts
  
> 1. Block clean acceptance on F1/F2/F4. Repair the shared outcome projection and secret/destination binding, then wire
 conservation to production request construction.
  2. Add torn-journal construction rollback/reopen proof, missed-SSE replay proof, and native IME default-action tests.
 Keep all core changes out of those fixes; separately escalate protected historical core deletion violations.
  3. Retain the successful deletion control-flow changes, but obtain disposable-fixture Windows recycle/readback eviden
ce before claiming real Shell recycling. Model post-recycle metadata failure explicitly.
  4. Resolve current root-suite failures without weakening sandbox/process-tree assertions; rerun all requested gates a
fter final product changes.
  
